<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umayyad Caliphate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Umayyad dynasty, Umayyads, Umayyad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> was the second Islamic caliphate, ruled by the Umayyad family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from 661 to 750 CE. It expanded Muslim rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley, and established Arabic as the language of administration. It was overthrown by the Abbasid revolution, though an Umayyad branch continued to rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate.jpg" alt="Umayyad Caliphate" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmed.Mhdv · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyad_Caliphate_740-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Abbasid Caliphate was founded in 750 CE following the Abbasid Revolution, which overthrew the <b>Umayyad dynasty</b> at the Battle of the Great Zab in January of that year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Abroad, they kept strained relations with the Ibadi Rustamids of Tahert, tense relations with the Zaydi Shi'ite Idrisids of Fez, and a hostile stance towards the <b>Umayyads</b> of Córdoba.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The caliphal title passed between <b>Umayyads</b> and the Hammudids, a Berber-backed dynasty of Idrisid descent, while governors, Saqaliba commanders and local notables seized power in cities such as Seville, Granada, Zaragoza, Valencia and Toledo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Emirate of Córdoba was founded by Abd al-Rahman ibn Muawiya, a grandson of the Umayyad caliph Hisham, who survived the Abbasid massacre of his family after the fall of the <b>Umayyad Caliphate</b> in 750.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the second half of the 8th century the far Maghreb had been effectively independent of the caliphs since the Kharijite-led Berber revolts of 739–40, and neither the <b>Umayyads</b> nor the Abbasids after 750 regained control of Morocco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Arab–Khazar wars of the seventh and eighth centuries the Khazars fought the <b>Umayyad Caliphate</b> across the Caucasus and destroyed an Arab army at Marj Ardabil in 730.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
